{
  "image_name": "62e372f3c1232e1c8ca384ad376b59d7.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure presents an energy-level picture of a molecular layer on a metal and explains how the vacuum-level shift at the interface is built from several contributions. Overall, it shows that when molecules adsorb on a metal, the original metal work function is modified by chemisorption and by interfacial dipoles, while the molecular ionization potential, electron affinity, and transport gap are referenced to a shifted vacuum level.\n\nThe main schematic compares a clean metal surface with a chemisorbed layer and illustrates frontier states associated with positive and negative integer charge transfer near the metal Fermi level. A formula at the top indicates that the total shift is decomposed into pushback, chemical, interface-dipole, and double-dipole terms, with a separate charge-transfer contribution.\n\nA second part highlights the origin of the double-dipole term: cations and anions distributed across the metal and surface layer create dipoles whose orientation changes the vacuum level upward or downward depending on which side carries positive or negative charge.\n\nThe last part suggests a design strategy: inserting a thin spacer between the metal and the molecular layer weakens direct coupling, alters the interface dipole pattern, and helps preserve the molecular electronic levels relative to the metal.",
